# Line-ending policy.
#
# This repo is worked on from Windows and from WSL against the same folder on an
# NTFS drive. Without this file the two disagree: Windows Git checks files out as
# CRLF while the blobs are LF, so Git on the WSL side reports every tracked file
# as modified. That produced an 84-file, 22k-line diff containing no actual
# changes, which buries real history and makes `git add -A` dangerous.
#
# `eol=lf` rather than plain `text=auto`: one representation everywhere is the
# only thing that stops the two checkouts flip-flopping. .NET, Angular, Docker and
# every editor in use here read LF fine.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts MUST stay LF regardless of platform. deploy-agent/entrypoint.sh
# and scripts/backup-postgres.sh run inside Linux containers, where a CRLF
# shebang makes the interpreter path unresolvable and the container exits with a
# "no such file or directory" that names a file which plainly exists.
*.sh text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.dockerignore text eol=lf

# Tools that genuinely want CRLF on Windows.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.sln text eol=crlf

# Never normalise these. Git's binary detection is good but an explicit rule
# costs nothing and a corrupted image is silent until someone opens it.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.eot binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.dll binary
*.exe binary
*.pfx binary
*.snk binary
